# homelab — initial planning notes
#
# captured 2026-08-25. discussion about picking a base OS for a
# KISS docker-based homelab on x86_64 desktop hardware. see also
# [[fedora]], [[docker]].


GOAL
----

# stated intent: a "just works" homelab running standard docker /
# docker-compose. no kubernetes, no orchestration layer, no distro
# gymnastics. one machine, ssh in, write docker-compose.yml files,
# stand up services like jellyfin / nextcloud / pi-hole / etc.
#
# hardware: an x86_64 desktop.


CANDIDATES CONSIDERED
---------------------

# the "just works" bracket for docker hosts basically comes down to
# three practical picks:
#
#   - Debian (stable)
#   - Ubuntu Server LTS
#   - (Fedora Server wasn't in the KISS shortlist for this exercise
#     — the debian-family options were the focus)


DEBIAN vs UBUNTU LTS — THE TRADEOFF
-----------------------------------

# both share the ".deb + apt + docker's official repo" workflow, so
# the differences are all about defaults, packaging quirks, and how
# much the OS editorializes.

# DEBIAN (stable):
#   + quiet defaults — no MOTD ads, no telemetry package, no snap
#   + /etc/network/interfaces is boring and predictable
#   + no cloud-init on the netinst path
#   + kernel + userland are fine once you're on the current stable
#   - historically the "stale packages" complaint applied; less true
#     on the newest release
#   - a shade less documentation coverage than ubuntu for homelab
#     tutorials, though for anything containerized it doesn't matter

# UBUNTU SERVER LTS (24.04):
#   + 5 years of security updates (10 with free-for-personal-use
#     Ubuntu Pro, capped at 5 machines)
#   + slightly newer packages than debian at release
#   + the deepest homelab tutorial pool of any distro — most guides
#     assume it
#   + HWE (hardware enablement) kernel available for newer silicon
#   - MOTD ads / "news" banners at login
#   - ubuntu-advantage-tools phones home by default
#   - netplan for networking (fine, just different)
#   - snap in the base install (doesn't affect docker itself since
#     you'd use docker's apt repo, but adds surface area)
#   - cloud-init can surprise you on server installs

# neither is "wrong". rough rule:
#   - prioritize documentation coverage → Ubuntu LTS
#   - prioritize a quiet, opinion-free base → Debian


DECISION
--------

# Debian 13 (trixie) on x86_64.
#
# trixie has been stable since August 2025, so as of 2026 it's
# firmly the current stable release (debian 12 / bookworm is now
# oldstable). trixie ships ~kernel 6.12 LTS + a newer everything,
# which closes most of the historical "debian is stale" gap.
#
# fits the KISS lean, doesn't editorialize, and the docker
# workflow is identical to what ubuntu would give (both go through
# docker's official apt repo either way).


PHASE 1 — BARE-METAL INSTALL
----------------------------

# grab the DEBIAN 13 (trixie) NETINST ISO. flash to a USB stick with
# BalenaEtcher, Rufus, or `dd` — small download, installer pulls the
# rest from mirrors at install time. boot from USB, step through the
# classic Debian Installer (ncurses; less glossy than ubuntu's
# subiquity, faster and less opinionated).


# FIRMWARE
# since debian 12, the netinst ISO bundles non-free firmware by
# default. most wi-fi / gpu / nic firmware works out of the box — no
# separate "unofficial firmware" ISO needed like in the pre-2023 days.


# NETWORKING
# the installer defaults to DHCP. pick "Configure network manually"
# and set a STATIC IP on your LAN's subnet — keeps the server's
# address stable across router reboots.
# alternate path: leave it on DHCP through install and lock the
# address down post-boot via /etc/network/interfaces. either works.


# ROOT PASSWORD (debian-specific gotcha)
# - if you SET a root password: root works normally, your regular
#   user has no sudo
# - if you LEAVE the root password BLANK: the installer disables
#   direct root login and adds your user to `sudo` automatically —
#   the ubuntu-style model
# for a KISS homelab where you'll sudo from your user account:
# LEAVE ROOT PASSWORD BLANK.


# PROFILE SETUP
# create your primary user: full name → short username → password
# (in that order in the installer flow).


# STORAGE
# "guided – use entire disk" is fine for a single-drive setup.
# debian does NOT offer ZFS-root in the installer; if you want
# ZFS / mdadm / LVM tricks, do them post-install or use manual
# partitioning.


# SOFTWARE SELECTION (tasksel)
# this is where the KISS win happens. deselect EVERYTHING except:
#   - SSH server
#   - standard system utilities
# no desktop, no print server (cups), no web server. yields a ~1 GB
# system with only the bits you need to be a docker host.


# DOCKER (post-first-boot)
# debian doesn't have snaps, so there's no snap prompt to skip. the
# equivalent trap is debian's own `docker.io` package — DON'T
# install it. it lags upstream by a couple of years and the
# compose-plugin story is fiddlier.
# after first boot, follow docs.docker.com/engine/install/debian to
# add docker's official apt repo and install `docker-ce` +
# `docker-compose-plugin` from there.
# then: add your user to the `docker` group (or set up rootless
# docker if you want extra isolation — the `docker` group is
# standard KISS) and start writing docker-compose.yml files.


PHASE 2 — FIRST ACCESS & FOUNDATION
-----------------------------------

# once the install finishes, reboot, unplug the monitor + keyboard,
# and walk away from the box. everything from here happens over ssh
# from the terminal (Command Prompt / PowerShell / Terminal) on
# your daily-driver computer.


# 1. SSH IN
# use the static IP you set in phase 1 (or, if you skipped that,
# `ip a` on the server console or the router's lease list will tell
# you what it grabbed):
ssh yourusername@<server-ip>

# first connection asks you to accept the host-key fingerprint —
# say yes. then, from your daily-driver, drop your public key onto
# the server so future logins skip the password prompt:
ssh-copy-id yourusername@<server-ip>

# once key auth works, harden /etc/ssh/sshd_config on the server:
#   PasswordAuthentication no
#   PermitRootLogin no
# then reload:
sudo systemctl reload ssh
# password brute-force attempts stop mattering.


# 2. UPDATE THE SYSTEM
# pull all the latest security patches:
sudo apt update && sudo apt full-upgrade -y

# debian-specific: use `apt full-upgrade`, NOT `apt upgrade`.
# full-upgrade will remove obsolete packages when needed to satisfy
# dependencies — matches what unattended-upgrades does under the
# hood. on a fresh netinst the two produce identical results, but
# the habit is worth building.
# if a new kernel came down, reboot:
sudo reboot


# 3. AUTO SECURITY UPDATES
# for a set-and-forget homelab, let debian apply security patches
# on its own:
sudo apt install unattended-upgrades
sudo dpkg-reconfigure --priority=low unattended-upgrades

# answer "yes" to enable. default config only pulls from the
# `security` archive — no risk of a random package-version bump
# breaking your compose stack overnight.


PHASE 3 — INSTALLING DOCKER & DOCKER COMPOSE
--------------------------------------------

# the engine that will run all your services. use the OFFICIAL
# UPSTREAM docker engine — NOT debian's own `docker.io` package
# (see phase 1's DOCKER note for the why).


# 1. TWO INSTALL PATHS — both official, both from docker.
#
# PATH A — convenience script (fast):
curl -fsSL https://get.docker.com -o get-docker.sh
sudo sh get-docker.sh
# one shot. docker warns this "isn't recommended for production" —
# for a KISS homelab it's fine, but it does mean running an
# internet-sourced shell script sight-unseen.
#
# PATH B — manual apt-repo (transparent):
# follow docs.docker.com/engine/install/debian — add docker's GPG
# key, add the apt repo, then:
sudo apt install docker-ce docker-ce-cli containerd.io \
    docker-buildx-plugin docker-compose-plugin
# more typing, but every step is visible and reviewable.
#
# either path leaves you with the same result: `docker` and
# `docker compose` (note: `compose` is now a plugin subcommand —
# NO hyphen. the standalone `docker-compose` script is deprecated).


# 2. RUN DOCKER WITHOUT SUDO
# by default every docker command needs sudo. add your user to the
# `docker` group so it doesn't:
sudo usermod -aG docker $USER
# log out of ssh and back in for the group change to take effect.
# verify:
docker run --rm hello-world
# should pull the image, print a hello message, and exit — all
# without sudo.


# SECURITY NOTE
# `docker` group membership is ROOT-EQUIVALENT — a container
# mounted with `-v /:/host` gets you root on the host. standard
# tradeoff for single-user homelabs. if you'd rather not, run
# ROOTLESS DOCKER (`dockerd-rootless-setuptool.sh install`) —
# docker runs entirely under your uid, no privileged daemon, at
# the cost of some networking / perf caveats.


PHASE 3.5 — HARDENING & CONVENTIONS
-----------------------------------

# quick set of things to nail down BEFORE running your first compose
# stack. every one of these is a "wish i'd done this at the start"
# item.


# 1. DOCKER LOG ROTATION (biggest silent gotcha)
# the default json-file log driver has NO rotation. one chatty
# container fills your disk over months. set global defaults once
# in /etc/docker/daemon.json:
sudo tee /etc/docker/daemon.json >/dev/null <<'JSON'
{
  "log-driver": "json-file",
  "log-opts": {
    "max-size": "10m",
    "max-file": "3"
  }
}
JSON
sudo systemctl restart docker
# per-container `logging:` blocks in compose still override these,
# but the daemon default kicks in for anything you forget to set.


# 2. TIME SYNC + TIMEZONE
# critical for TLS cert validation, cron jobs, and correlating logs
# across services. debian 13 ships systemd-timesyncd enabled by
# default — verify:
timedatectl status
# should show `System clock synchronized: yes` and
# `NTP service: active`.
#
# if the installer left you on UTC and you want local time in logs:
sudo timedatectl set-timezone Region/City
# (`timedatectl list-timezones` shows the full list.)


# 3. FIREWALL (ufw)
# lock the box down to just ssh before you start publishing docker
# ports:
sudo apt install ufw
sudo ufw allow OpenSSH
sudo ufw enable
sudo ufw status
#
# GOTCHA: docker's iptables rules bypass ufw by design. every port
# you publish with `ports:` in a compose file is exposed regardless
# of ufw's rules. this is intentional (docker "just works" for the
# user) but it means ufw only really protects services that AREN'T
# containerized. mitigations:
#   - bind published ports to loopback in compose
#     (`127.0.0.1:8080:80`) so only a reverse proxy sees them
#   - or use the `ufw-docker` community tool that patches ufw to
#     actually see docker traffic
# for a LAN-only homelab where you trust everything on your subnet,
# the default is fine — just know the constraint.


# 4. FILESYSTEM CONVENTION FOR COMPOSE FILES + DATA
# pick this once. every service you add uses the same layout, and
# backup planning gets trivial. common pattern:
#
#   /opt/stacks/<service>/docker-compose.yml
#   /opt/stacks/<service>/data/         # bind mounts live here
#   /opt/stacks/<service>/.env          # secrets, gitignored
#
# variant: ~/stacks/<service>/ under your home dir if you don't
# want root-owned service files. picks a fight with the `docker`
# group's root-equivalence in a different way, but works.
#
# whatever you pick, be consistent — future-you doing a backup or
# a migration will thank present-you.


